@prefix pm:      <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/ontology#> .
@prefix pmc:     <https://pajew.ski/prima-materia/concepts/> .
@prefix rdfs:    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#> .
@prefix skos:    <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#> .
@prefix xsd:     <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

#
# prima-materia — where the handbook puts the one test it can fail
#
# One generalization, formed on 2026-09-22 after the appendix of the great
# Paris papyrus was read in the edition. That appendix has a test of the plainest
# kind — smear the juice on the black tip of an ibis feather and the barbs fall
# off at a touch — and it is a test of a plant, while the far-shining sight, the
# apparition and the immortalization that the plant serves have none. The
# holding already carried the same arrangement from a second handbook of the
# same find and had recorded it as a remark at one node. Setting the two beside
# the two cases where a handbook does test its own operation is what produced
# the statement, and the useful half is again the other side: the operation is
# tested, and the failing outcome is then taken back in the same sentence.
#

pmc:TheTestOnTheIngredient a pm:Generalizing ;
    rdfs:label "The test a handbook can fail is a test of what it buys, not of what it does"@en, "Die Probe, die ein Handbuch durchfallen kann, prueft, was es einkauft, und nicht, was es tut"@de ;
    pm:generalizedStatement "Where an operative handbook states a procedure of assay — an act with a definite perceptible outcome by which something is established — the thing assayed is a material the operation consumes and not the operation itself, and the assay is stated without any clause that would excuse a negative result. Where such a handbook does state an assay of its own operation, it attaches in the same passage a clause that removes the negative result, either by prescribing repetition of the operation until the outcome arrives or by assigning the absent outcome to a cause outside the procedure. The statement is about assays a single practitioner performs on his own materials or his own work. It does not govern a contest in which two practitioners measure their capacities against one another, nor a divination whose outcome is the answer sought rather than a verdict on the procedure; both of those are held elsewhere in this holding and both do state criteria on two sides."@en ;
    pm:generalizedFrom pmc:PgmIvProbatioKentritidis, pmc:PdmProbatioLapidis, pmc:JinchengYaocheng, pmc:YinshuKouchi ;
    pm:attestedBy pm:compilerInference ;
    skos:note "What each witness supplies, in the transmission's own terms. The great Paris papyrus gives the recognition of the herb its operation depends on: the black tip of an ibis feather is slackened and smeared with the juice and at the mere touching the barbs fall off. That is stated flat, with no alternative and no excuse, and it decides the identity of the plant. The same appendix states of the far-shining sight only that the man anointed will see so that the operator marvels, and adds that no greater operation was found in the world. The demotic handbook of the same find gives the way to know that the white stone is genuine: a little is ground with water and rubbed on a man's skin for a short time, and it takes the skin off. The same work's divinations have seven or eight announced signs of arrival and, as this holding has already recorded, not one verdict on the operation. Against these the fourth chapter of the Baopuzi, which does assay the elixir: elixir joined with mercury and put to the fire makes gold, and where the gold forms the drug is complete — and where it does not form, the drug is to be sealed and fired again for the same count of days, after which, the chapter says, there is no case in which it does not form. And the Pulling Book, whose preventive states an outcome anybody could look for in his own mouth and then says, of the case in which the outcome is missing, to do more of the same."@en ;
    skos:note "Why the two limbs belong in one statement and not in two. An ingredient is bought, found or grown, so a failing assay of it costs the practitioner a specimen and leaves the procedure untouched; an assay of the operation would cost the procedure its standing. The statement says that handbooks are exact in the first place and provided-for in the second, and it is the pair that carries the claim. A holding that only recorded the immunising clauses would be reporting that these transmissions cannot state a testable thing, and that is false: they can, and they do it where it is safe."@en ;
    skos:note "Counter-search, carried against the compiled holding before the statement was written, as SPEC section 16 requires. Every node whose definition states an assay, a way of knowing, a genuine article or a counterfeit was read. Three classes of near-miss and no refutation. The ordeals and the divinatory criteria that this holding carries in quantity — the sinking or floating in boiling water, the corroborative second consultation of the poison oracle, the fork at the shaman's seance, the recovery of the sick after the immersion, the felling of the ordeal tree — all state both sides, and they were the material of pmc:TheCriterionNoPartyControls. They are excluded by the wording and not by exception: in each the outcome is the answer that was asked for, not a verdict on whether the procedure was done correctly. The contests, which the same neighbouring statement already separates out, are excluded on the same ground. And the discernment literature, which teaches that a consolation may be counterfeit and is recognised by its circumstances, names no act with an outcome and so states no assay at all."@en ;
    skos:note "Two cases were checked as possible refutations and are not. The signs of verification of the internal alchemy do state what success looks like, and the same chapter answers the practitioner who asks what happens if it does not come by naming two defects in the man — his choice of teacher and his knowledge of the times — which is the second limb and not a counter-case. The Egyptian gate material makes knowing the names the condition of passage and says a dozen times over what happens to the man who does not know, which looks like an assay of the operation until one asks who would observe it: the outcome falls after death and no bystander is given a criterion."@en ;
    skos:note "Thin where it is thin, and the thinness is on the first limb. Two of its four witnesses are two works of one corpus, found together and written within a century or two of one another, so for the first limb this holding has one tradition and not two. That is the reason the protocol below is entered as a procedure without cases rather than as cases without deviation: what has been established is that the arrangement exists and is not accidental to one manuscript, not that it is the normal case."@en ;
    skos:note "Refuted in its second limb on 2026-09-22, three days after it was written, and the case is recorded rather than the statement quietly repaired. The Romanus-Büchlein, opened for the first time in this holding, prints after its third blood-staunching the sentence: but whoever will not believe it, let him write the letters on a knife and stab an unreasoning animal, and it will not bleed. That is an assay of the operation itself, with an outcome that could come out the other way, and the booklet attaches to the refuting outcome neither a rule of repetition nor a cause lying outside the procedure. The node is pmc:RomanusProbaAdIncredulum and the protocol below carries the deviation."@en ;
    skos:note "What survives and what does not. The first limb stands: the assays of materials in this holding are still exact and still carry no excuse. The second limb does not stand as a universal, and the successor statement will have to say less — that where a handbook assays its own operation it usually attaches an escape, and that the exception is addressed to a doubter rather than to the operator. That last part is the interesting residue: the two clauses in this holding that put an operation at risk are both demonstrations offered to somebody who does not believe, and neither is an instruction to the practitioner. Whether that is the rule was not established and is the question the protocol now carries."@en ;
    skos:note "A third demonstration of the same shape was read on 2026-09-22, in a corpus as far from the first two as the register reaches, and it bears on the residue rather than on the statement. An American healer receives a patient who has come after hearing him called a spiritualist and a mesmerist, tells her he never wishes a patient to tell him her feelings, takes her hand, names a sharp pain in one vertebra and a pain in the left temple affecting the sight of that eye, and only then explains his theory. Like the demotic assay and like the booklet's knife, it is offered to somebody who does not believe, it can come out the other way, and no clause covers the outcome in which it does. The node is pmc:QuimbyProbaAdAegrum. Three cases from three transmissions with no route between them is no longer a coincidence of two manuscripts, and the question the protocol carries — whether every assay of an operation in these corpora is aimed at a sceptic — now has a third case supporting it and still no count."@en ;
    skos:note "The same run produced a fourth outcome the first limb does not provide for, and it is recorded because the successor statement will have to handle it. The first limb says a handbook assays its materials and finds them genuine or counterfeit. This healer assayed a material of his own — he believed silk attracted his subject, and found the subject's hand moved toward a lady with a silk apron even when she stood behind him — and concluded neither that the silk was good nor that it was false, but that his own belief had produced the effect and that no material had been involved. An assay whose result is that the assayed thing was never in the procedure is a third result, and it moves the variable into the operator's mind, where nothing further can reach it. The node is pmc:QuimbySericumOperantis."@en ;
    skos:note "It was refuted from the place the falsifier did not name. The falsifier sent a later run to the pharmacological and alchemical literature; the counter-case came from a booklet of household charms that this file had cited three times without opening. That is worth recording about the falsifier and not only about the statement: the corpus most likely to refute a claim about handbooks was the handbook the holding already had a citation to."@en ;
    skos:note "The address the falsifier named was opened on 2026-09-25 and it paid, on both limbs. The mercurial alchemy of India was registered and unopened when the sentence below was written; the first volume of Mookerjee's Rasa-Jala-Nidhi has now been read at the wording, and it supplies a witness for the first limb and a second counter-case for the second. First limb: the look of purified mercury, deep blue inside and bright as the mid-day sun outside, decides the material and carries no excuse, and the failing material goes back through the operation. Second limb: the five marks of incinerated mercury — lustreless, light, white, a killer of metals, devoid of subtleness, and it does not sublimate if placed upon fire — are an assay of the handbook's own sixteenth operation, and the volume names the failing outcome in its own right and gives it a verdict: mercury that sublimates out of the crucible during the incineration is called sajiva baddha, does not possess the properties of incinerated mercury, and is not efficacious. No repetition rule and no external cause stands beside it. The nodes are pmc:RasaSuddharasalaksana and pmc:RasaBhasmalaksana in traditions/rasashastra.ttl."@en ;
    skos:note "This second counter-case settles the residue the file had been carrying, and it settles it against the guess. After the Romanus-Büchlein and the American healer the open question was whether every assay of an operation in these corpora is addressed to somebody who does not believe. This one is not. It stands in a chapter of definitions between the twenty-five classes of solidified mercury, it is addressed to the operator at his own bench, and the person it would disappoint is the man who did the work. The successor statement therefore cannot be rescued by restricting it to demonstrations offered to sceptics, and the second limb is not weakened but simply gone."@en ;
    skos:note "A third thing the same volume shows, and it belongs beside the second limb rather than against it: the handbook does assay its own operation with a rule of repetition where the material allows one. After the swallowing of gold the mercury is to weigh what it weighed before, and if it weighs more it is to be boiled again until it reverts to its original weight. So one work carries both arrangements — an assay of the operation with a repetition rule at the tenth operation, and an assay of the operation without one at the sixteenth — which is the sharpest evidence in the holding that the device is chosen case by case and is not a property of the genre. The node is pmc:RasaGrasanaParyaptiPariksa."@en ;
    skos:note "Falsified by an operative handbook that states, of its own operation, an act with a definite perceptible outcome by which the operation is established to have worked or not worked, and attaches to the negative outcome neither a repetition rule nor a cause outside the procedure. One such case refutes it. The likeliest place to look is the pharmacological and alchemical literature rather than the ritual, because a text that already assays its ingredients has the form to hand: the Leiden papyrus of the same find stands unopened at pmw:PgmXII in traditions/greek-magical-papyri.ttl, and of the two corpora this sentence named as unopened the second, the mercurial alchemy of India, has since been opened and produced the case recorded above."@en .

pmc:TheTestOnTheIngredientExamination a pm:Testing ;
    rdfs:label "Protocol on where the handbook puts the test it can fail"@en, "Pruefprotokoll dazu, wohin das Handbuch die Probe legt, die es durchfallen kann"@de ;
    skos:definition "An attempt to fail the statement that an operative handbook assays its materials and not its operation. The protocol is a reading protocol and not a field one: what is claimed is a regularity in what handbooks say, so it fails or holds against texts."@en ;
    skos:note "Moved from procedure without cases to cases with deviation on 2026-09-22. One corpus has been run through with the question and it produced one case, and the case is against the statement's second limb. That is a single case and it does not measure how common the arrangement is; what it does is remove the universal, which is all a single case can do and all this protocol claims."@en ;
    skos:note "A second corpus was run through with the same question on 2026-09-25, the first volume of the Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, three hundred and fifty pages read at the wording. It produced three cases at once: one for the first limb, one against the second, and one for the second, all three in the same work. Two counter-cases from two corpora with no route between them are no longer a property of one booklet; the proportion this protocol wants is still not measured, and what is measured now is that both arrangements occur inside a single handbook."@en ;
    skos:note "The first corpus the protocol named as its next step, the second bilingual handbook of the same Egyptian find, is still unopened and is still the right next step — now for a different question. The universal is gone; what is open is the proportion, and for that the protocol needs corpora counted rather than corpora sampled."@en ;
    skos:note "Entered at procedure without cases and not at no procedure devised. The procedure exists and is stated in the falsifier: open a handbook that prescribes operations with materials, list every act it states with a definite perceptible outcome, and for each one ask whether the thing decided is a material or the operation, and if the operation, whether a repetition rule or an external cause stands beside it. Nobody has yet gone through a corpus with that question; what has been done is a sweep of the nodes already entered, which is a sweep of what earlier runs happened to find worth entering and not a count."@en ;
    skos:note "The first corpus to run it against is named at the generalization and is unopened: the second bilingual handbook of the same find. That is a good first case because it is the nearest neighbour of the work that supplies the second witness, so a divergence there would be the cheapest possible refutation and a concurrence the cheapest possible strengthening — and because a handbook of the same milieu removes the objection that the arrangement belongs to a genre rather than to the transmissions."@en ;
    pm:tests pmc:TheTestOnTheIngredient ;
    pm:examinationState pm:casesWithDeviation ;
    pm:counterSearch pm:counterSearchFoundOpposition ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched on 2026-09-22 in the Romanus-Büchlein, read at the wording throughout in the reprint in Scheible, Das Kloster III (Stuttgart 1846), pp. 489-523, for any act with a definite perceptible outcome by which one of its own operations is established, and for whatever clause stands beside such an outcome. One case came back and it opposes the statement: the demonstration against disbelief at the third blood-staunching, p. 503, which stabs an animal and predicts that it will not bleed, with nothing attached to the case in which it does. Two further passages were read as candidates and are not counter-cases: the shepherd's trial at pp. 511-512 vouches for a substitution of apparatus and states no outcome, and the ninth-day term for a broken leg at pp. 518-519 is a deadline on an effect and not an act performed to establish anything."@en ;
    pm:counterSearchNote "Searched again on 2026-09-25 in Bhudeb Mookerjee, Rasa-Jala-Nidhi, vol. I, Calcutta 1926, read at the wording throughout, for every act with a definite perceptible outcome and for the clause standing beside it. Six were found and they divide. Assays of a material, no excuse attached: the look of purified mercury, p. 38, and the four appearances of the swallowed morsels, p. 140. Assay of the operation with a repetition rule: the weight test after the swallowing of gold, pp. 86 and 134, boil it again until the weight reverts. Assay of the operation with no rule and no external cause: the five marks of incinerated mercury, p. 175, with the named failing class sajiva baddha at p. 314, which is declared not to possess the properties of incinerated mercury and not to be efficacious. That one opposes the statement's second limb and is the second case of its kind in this protocol. Also inspected and not counter-cases: the grass test for the heat of the sand apparatus, p. 259, which establishes a heat and not an operation, and the red colour seen through the flask at p. 100, which is an endpoint and not a verdict."@en ;
    pm:falsifiedBy "An operative handbook that states, of its own operation, an act with a definite perceptible outcome by which the operation is established to have worked or not to have worked, and attaches to the negative outcome neither a rule of repetition nor a cause lying outside the procedure."@en ;
    pm:examinedBy "prima-materia, protocol drawn from the wording of the four witnesses and from a sweep of the holding's assay nodes; two corpora since read through with the question, the Romanus-Büchlein on 2026-09-22 and the first volume of the Rasa-Jala-Nidhi on 2026-09-25" ;
    pm:protocolUpdated "2026-09-25"^^xsd:date .
